A||N||4||fiero651||86 SE V6 no start, very frustrated, HELP!!||||11||
Z||000000||fiero651||05-22-2001||11:10 AM||||OK I have had my Fiero in storage so it has not run for about 8-10 months.  But when it was parked it ran except it needed a new water pump, which I have replaced.  Now I want to bring it out and the thing will not fire. It cranks but it won't turn over. I checked to best of my knowledge there was no spark.  So I figured I would start with the ignition module, no luck there.  Then the coil, still no luck.  I took the cap off and saw a little corrosion so I sanded it off and then popped the rotor off and cleaned the terminal on the bottom of that and the post coming up from the distributor (both were rusty) still nothing.  I can smell gas though after cranking so I am pretty sure I am getting some fuel.  I checked all the wire connections and they seem tight and the spark plug wires were relatively new when I parked the car.  Please help me I am running out of suggestions.  Also I took the dist. cap off more than once and the rotor was pointing a different way so I would say that it is spining.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||11:18 AM||||Take the spark plug wire from the coil that goes to the center of the distributor off the distributor and put a screwdriver in it and see if that has spark.<P>  If there is no spark there then that wire itself is bad - or something is wrong with the coil / pickup coil / ignition control module.<P> If you have spark there then pull your plugs and look at them - if they look fouled at all replace them.<P>  If the plugs are ok then that leaves the plug wires, or there is moisture in the cap.<P>  also - just for a sanity check - make sure the plug wires are on in the proper order - maybe someone is playing a joke on  you or messed with the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MooCow||05-22-2001||11:50 AM||||I had the same problem with mine.  It ended up being the pickup coil inside of teh distributor.  Make sure you do this right or your timming will get all messed up like mine did.  Go here and it will tell you how to remove the pickup coil. Here <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html</A> <BR>Good luck<BR>                               Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Topknot||05-22-2001||03:34 PM||||Hallo fiero651,<P>Well if your wanting to fire it back to life and even thou you "cleaned" the distributor parts..why not just go out and get a new cap, rotor, plugs..etc...not that $$$ and gives you the knowledge of them being new.<P>Also preform a clean out oil change if able to and put some fresh gas in..being that gas will breakdown after a length of time.<P>If you know for a fact that the fullpump is pumping fuel!  Normally a sitting car should be started at least twice a month to keep things flowing.  Like the GT 86 I am working on..it was stored for 4 years, so things like the clutch shifter and such needed oiling and the brakes and rotors are rusted, althou a bit of driving will shine them up to a point, just gonna buy new pads and rotors and start fresh.<P>Just my 3 cents worth!<P>Topknot =]<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||beer_stud_21||05-22-2001||11:24 PM||||You can use a multimeter to test the pick-up coil.  The instructions are in the hanes manual.  That is what was the cause of the lack of spark in my 85GT.  You have to pull the distributor, but its not that hard of a job.  I've done it enough times in the last couple months I could do it with my eyes closed.  If you need the instructions on testing the pickup coil let me know and I will post them.  Testing it while the distributor is in the car sure beats pulling a working one just to find out it wasnt you problem.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
